WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.100
Film Courage: We had some Youtube questions
come in earlier and this is from Harry ML

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:09.300
(that’s the user name) “How to make characters
consistent, but act in surprising ways?”

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:12.570
Gordy Hoffman, Founder and Judge of the BlueCat
Screenplay Competition: You know writers have

00:00:12.570 --> 00:00:18.090
impulses inside them that they often don’t
follow because they want to stay on track

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:27.570
with an outline or they want to stay on track
with a beat sheet or a plan or they have expectations

00:00:27.570 --> 00:00:33.360
for what is appropriate for a certain story
or a kind of genre that they’re writing

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:40.820
and so they ignore the impulses or the intuitive
thoughts that they have.

00:00:40.820 --> 00:00:50.210
The imagination sparks and those are the things
when you surprise yourself with an idea you

00:00:50.210 --> 00:00:52.220
need to embrace it.

00:00:52.220 --> 00:00:55.810
Whenever something comes up in your mind.

00:00:55.810 --> 00:01:02.150
You know we self-censor a lot and we’re
like “Well, he can’t rip her shirt off

00:01:02.150 --> 00:01:03.510
right there, can he?”

00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:08.790
And it’s just like “No it’s got to be
something else.”

00:01:08.790 --> 00:01:11.500
And it’s just like “Maybe he does?”

00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:15.950
And maybe she grabs his wrists after that
because then they’re like “Then what happens?

00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:17.000
Then what happens?

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:18.000
Then what happens?”

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:21.840
Then you are going down the rabbit hole and
you’re making things up and you’re surprising

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:25.620
yourself, that’s how we create characters.

00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:30.370
Obviously we want the character to still be
the character.

00:01:30.370 --> 00:01:35.390
Would the character maybe want to rip the
dress, rip the shirt off or whatever.

00:01:35.390 --> 00:01:39.860
Or grab the paint can or run out of the car
and start running down the field when they

00:01:39.860 --> 00:01:44.030
were supposed to stay in the car in the outline
and then they started running toward the football

00:01:44.030 --> 00:01:52.970
field when you started to write it, are you
going to allow that to happen?

00:01:52.970 --> 00:01:56.479
Writers have to listen to their instrument,
listen to their imagination.

00:01:56.479 --> 00:02:00.380
You know you don’t talk about imagination
a lot and when we talk about the craft of

00:02:00.380 --> 00:02:04.549
screenwriting we don’t really talk about
imagination.

00:02:04.549 --> 00:02:09.619
Like what that is and how that is…like what
if (that whole thing)?

00:02:09.619 --> 00:02:16.120
And we don’t cultivate that because it’s
really so messy and so non-linear and spontaneous

00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:18.010
that it’s hard to write a book about it.

00:02:18.010 --> 00:02:25.060
It’s hard to organize a system, a beat sheet
or a formula about imagination.

00:02:25.060 --> 00:02:29.810
But that is really is how you create on surprising
revelatory behavior.

00:02:29.810 --> 00:02:36.800
It’s sort of like and then what if this
happens and you’re just like “Oh my gosh!

00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:44.930
We were talking about THE MEG and how people
are just like “I’m not going to see this

00:02:44.930 --> 00:02:50.350
shark movie with Jason Statham and it’s
a genre movie, like a silly, stupid movie,

00:02:50.350 --> 00:02:51.350
right?”

00:02:51.350 --> 00:02:56.050
But there were a couple of moments in there
that were complexly surprising and I thought

00:02:56.050 --> 00:02:57.050
“A-plus!”

00:02:57.050 --> 00:03:03.599
That’s stuff that doesn’t happen in movies
where it’s like the audience completely

00:03:03.599 --> 00:03:11.610
fell into a lull and then the plot went left
and it wasn’t like “Oh give me a break?”

00:03:11.610 --> 00:03:19.430
It was like “This is plausible and it’s
arresting and it’s a surprise and it very

00:03:19.430 --> 00:03:29.310
likely probably came from the imagination
of their author going well and then they thought

00:03:29.310 --> 00:03:31.420
of it “Why not!

00:03:31.420 --> 00:03:32.990
Let’s try that.”

00:03:32.990 --> 00:03:37.490
And embracing it because initially you come
up with something like that and you go “Oohh!!”

00:03:37.490 --> 00:03:39.900
And an audience is like “What?”

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:46.700
And so I think that’s what it is, it’s
like painting attention to your own impulses

00:03:46.700 --> 00:03:49.110
and listening to your own voice.

00:03:49.110 --> 00:03:57.349
You listen to your own voice and let it be
the primary thing in the creative act of your

00:03:57.349 --> 00:04:01.910
script then that will allow for these surprises
to happen.

00:04:01.910 --> 00:04:05.019
Film Courage: It reminds me of when SHARP
OBJECTS first came out.

00:04:05.019 --> 00:04:09.849
I didn’t see it when it initially came out
but I watched people’s reactions on Twitter

00:04:09.849 --> 00:04:13.500
and by seeing this then I was like “Okay
I want to watch it.”

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:18.129
And they were shocked and let’s assume these
were real Twitter accounts people couldn’t

00:04:18.129 --> 00:04:19.129
believe it.

00:04:19.129 --> 00:04:23.389
And Amy Adams can play so many amazing characters
but she was playing this damaged character

00:04:23.389 --> 00:04:25.949
that was so fun to watch.

00:04:25.949 --> 00:04:27.110
Gordy: Right!

00:04:27.110 --> 00:04:36.840
So absolutely and that’s what we want to
do and that all comes from someone allowing

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:38.770
themselves to be creative.

00:04:38.770 --> 00:04:44.979
You’ve got to say like “You know, I’m
going to have her like run out of the car.

00:04:44.979 --> 00:04:53.729
And that’s going to totally screw up my
outline but it’s really cool but maybe I

00:04:53.729 --> 00:04:58.180
could have a lot more work to do because I’m
making this decision.

00:04:58.180 --> 00:05:04.349
But I’d rather do a lot more work and not
be lazy and embrace it because it’s really

00:05:04.349 --> 00:05:08.139
revelatory and awesome but people don’t
want to do that.

00:05:08.139 --> 00:05:12.909
They’re like if I ever do that then everything
is screwed up and so I’m just going to stay

00:05:12.909 --> 00:05:19.749
on course on the thing that’s not surprising
and people are predicting because I’m predicting

00:05:19.749 --> 00:05:22.860
everything because I’m following a road
map.

00:05:22.860 --> 00:05:27.590
You lose the road map and you start flying
by the seat of your pants that’s like “What’s

00:05:27.590 --> 00:05:28.590
going to happen?

00:05:28.590 --> 00:05:30.020
I don’t know what’s going to happen?”

00:05:30.020 --> 00:05:32.999
Film Courage: Do you think that’s really
laziness or that’s again wanting to be the

00:05:32.999 --> 00:05:37.189
teacher’s pet versus the Johnny Rotten in
the back of the room?

00:05:37.189 --> 00:05:38.210
Gordy: Well of course it’s fear.

00:05:38.210 --> 00:05:43.969
It’s fear of I need to do it this way or
it’s not going to work out but I think mostly

00:05:43.969 --> 00:05:51.779
writers they won’t upend their outline or
they won’t upend their plans or whatever.

00:05:51.779 --> 00:05:56.889
I mean a lot of them will say “Well look
if it goes off my script, I always embrace

00:05:56.889 --> 00:06:00.009
it,” but I don’t know.

00:06:00.009 --> 00:06:02.789
But I think a lot of people.

00:06:02.789 --> 00:06:12.059
I think we write outlines a lot of times to
make the process faster but at the expense

00:06:12.059 --> 00:06:15.599
of the something being more surprising and
revelatory.

00:06:15.599 --> 00:06:19.860
A lot of things don’t need to be outlined
if they’re character-driven, you can sort

00:06:19.860 --> 00:06:27.000
of discover a lot really special moments in
a story by just seeing where it goes.

00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:35.050
A heist movie, a mystery, that becomes more
difficult to just follow impulses because

00:06:35.050 --> 00:06:37.619
you’ve got more of a puzzle to figure out.

00:06:37.619 --> 00:06:40.169
It’s like “Okay, how is this going to
work?”

00:06:40.169 --> 00:06:40.729
But…you know…

